sible by3 in unordered pairs, we got1928. Now which one is correct.\n\nWait, perhaps the way ordered pair inclusion-exclusion was calculated applies to unordered pairs as well. Let me see.\n\nSuppose in ordered pair incl-excl sum, the counts sum_a(86 -a )=1190 and sum_b(b -1 )=1190. These counts when converted to unordered pairs would be counted as pairs. For example, for a=3i, number of pairs is sum over a=3i, b= any >a: which in ordered pairs is86 -3i. In unordered pairs, the number of pairs is the number of b\'s such that3i <b ≤86. So for each a=3i, the number of b\'s isfloor(86/1 ) -3i if3i <86. So sum_{a divisible by3}(number of b\'s in unordered pairs)=Sum_{i=1}^{28} (86 -3i )=1190, which coincides with the ordered pairs sum. Similarly, sum over b divisible by3: sum_{j=1}^{28}(b -1 ) where b=3j. In ordered pairs, this is sum_{j=1}^{28}(3j -1 )=1190. In unordered pairs, the number of a\'s for each b=3j is (b -1 )=3j -1. But in ordered pairs, this is counted as part of the sum. Hence, the inclusion-exclusion approach when applied to ordered pairs gives counts that correspond to unordered pairs. Hence, perhaps the correct answer is1928. But wait, complementary approach says2087.\n\nThe difference may lie in the way overlapping pairs are counted. For example, in the incl-excl on ordered pairs, the overlap counts pairs where both a and b divisible by3, which in unordered pairs would be each pair counted once. Hence, the inclusion-exclusion in ordered pairs already accounts for unordered pairs correctly.\n\nBut in the complementary approach, the count of pairs where neither a nor b divisible by3 may be wrong. How is that?\n\nNumber of numbers not divisible by3:58. So in unordered pairs, the number of pairs isC(58,2 ) for unordered pairs. But wait, in ordered pairs, the analogous count would be:\n\nSum_{a not divisible by3} Sum_{b not divisible by3 and a <b} 1.\n\nWhich is different.\n\nWait, the confusion is in how ordered and unordered pair counts differ. For the c